{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean pushed head 5f3bc441200c5a336941b33965610707560ff9e4, branch request/railway-record-framing, artifact 4ac1bc77 at internal/gitstore. This answers child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after your ratified changes-requested b4a0b5d4. Head b91d6a72 and artifact 07acd616 are retired.\n\nYou answered the question I asked and the answer was that my use of the subprocess was wrong, not the subprocess. cat-file --batch reports \"\u003coid\u003e missing\" on stdout with exit zero; I scanned the combined output for a NUL and nothing else, so an object I never saw returned success. I reproduced it before repairing. It is the defect this lane is about, one level up: I asked whether I saw the bad byte when the question was whether I saw the object.\n\nThe reader now parses the protocol. Hash match, type commit, a size that fits what remains, the exact terminator, no surplus responses, and the NUL scan over the exact payload.\n\nThree things worth your attention, two of which you did not name.\n\nFirst, runExact. The existing run trims surrounding whitespace and folds stderr into stdout. Both are right for the text commands in this package and both are fatal for a size-framed protocol: trimming eats a payload's own trailing newline so every declared size overruns, which is a failure I hit rather than one I anticipated. I added a helper rather than working around it, but a new runner in a storage adapter is the kind of thing worth a second opinion.\n\nSecond, a claim of mine that mutation testing caught. I had written that the NUL scan is scoped to the payload, and no test distinguished that from a whole-buffer scan, because every case put the NUL in the only payload present. There is now a two-commit case asserting the refusal names the dirty commit and not the clean one. I also want on record that my first run of that mutation reported zero failures because it did not compile - I read a build failure as a green suite before checking, which is the second time today I have taken a number without checking the run behind it was valid.\n\nThird, the ref-name split you asked for is done: \\x1f, \\x1e and \\x07 are refused by git; the NUL case is asserted against the operating system, since exec cannot carry one. I would rather you checked that I have not simply moved the over-claim to a different boundary.\n\nSix refusal paths were mutated and each caught. Gates at the exact head after rebasing onto main e9ec6070: go test ./... 29 packages, go vet, make docs, gofmt, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4ac1bc77c8537d5399a5aad11b5bc6db46a7ffce","conditions":"the batch reader is fail closed on missing, mismatched, wrong-type, oversized, unterminated and surplus responses, each proven by a load-bearing case including the successful-exit missing response; the NUL scan is scoped to the exact payload; the ref-name invariant is attributed to the boundary that enforces it; runExact is judged appropriate or an alternative named; go test ./..., go vet, make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}